server: do not treat the desktop window as a foreground process.

A Wine application cannot bring its own window to the front once the input
focus has moved to a window that does not belong to Wine. SetForegroundWindow
returns 0 and sets ERROR_ACCESS_DENIED. Under a compositor this is every
file-manager double-click: the user is in Dolphin, the running application is
asked to open a document, and it cannot come forward to show it.

The denial comes from the foreground permission check in
req_set_foreground_window:

    if (!is_current_process_foreground( desktop ) && queue->input &&
        desktop->foreground_input &&
        queue->input->user_time < desktop->foreground_input->user_time)
        set_error( STATUS_ACCESS_DENIED );

When the focus leaves Wine, the graphics driver makes the desktop window the
foreground window -- on X11 through the _NET_ACTIVE_WINDOW tracking in
WM_WINE_WINDOW_STATE_CHANGED, which calls set_foreground_window(..., internal).
set_foreground_input then records the desktop's owner, explorer.exe, as
desktop->foreground_pid and stamps its thread input with the current time.

Both are wrong. explorer.exe was not activated and received no user input; the
focus merely left Wine altogether. But the effect is that every other process
now fails is_current_process_foreground(), and every user_time in the desktop
is older than the stamp that was just written, so the check denies everyone --
including the application the user was working in a moment earlier.

The request path already gets this right: when a non-internal request targets
the desktop window it passes a null input, and a null desktop->foreground_input
short-circuits the check. Only the driver's internal requests set the trap.
So treat both the same way: when the target is the desktop window, do not
record a foreground process and do not stamp a user-input time that never
happened. desktop->foreground_pid == 0 already means "no foreground process"
to is_current_process_foreground(), which matches the documented Windows rule
that SetForegroundWindow succeeds when there is no foreground window.

The window manager remains the arbiter of whether the raise is honoured: the
driver still asks for it with _NET_ACTIVE_WINDOW and focus-stealing prevention
applies as before.

Measured on Wine 11.16, Plasma 6 on Wayland (Wine under XWayland), with a
test program that takes the foreground, loses it to a non-Wine window, and
then calls SetForegroundWindow on its own window:

  unpatched   ret=0 err=5 (ACCESS_DENIED), window never activated  (2/2 runs)
  patched     ret=1, _NET_ACTIVE_WINDOW granted, window activated  (3/3 runs)

--- a/server/queue.c
+++ b/server/queue.c
@@ -641,19 +641,35 @@
 }
 
 /* change the foreground input and reset the cursor clip rect */
-static void set_foreground_input( struct desktop *desktop, struct process *process, struct thread_input *input )
+static void set_foreground_input( struct desktop *desktop, struct process *process,
+                                  struct thread_input *input, int is_desktop )
 {
     input_shm_t *input_shm, *old_input_shm;
     shared_object_t dummy_obj = {0};
 
-    if (input) input->user_time = monotonic_time;
-    if (desktop->foreground_input == input) return;
+    /* Making the desktop window foreground means that no application window is
+     * foreground.  On Unix the graphics driver does this whenever the input
+     * focus moves to a window that does not belong to Wine at all, and then the
+     * desktop's owner is not a foreground process in any meaningful sense: it
+     * has not been activated and it has received no user input.  Recording it as
+     * one, and stamping its input with the current time, makes every application
+     * fail the foreground permission check below for as long as the focus stays
+     * outside Wine -- including the application the user was working in a moment
+     * earlier, which can then no longer bring itself back to the front.
+     * Requests that come from the application side already avoid this by passing
+     * a null input; do the same for the driver's internal ones. */
+    if (input && !is_desktop) input->user_time = monotonic_time;
+    if (desktop->foreground_input == input)
+    {
+        desktop->foreground_pid = is_desktop ? 0 : process->id;
+        return;
+    }
     input_shm = input ? input->shared : &dummy_obj.shm.input;
     old_input_shm = desktop->foreground_input ? desktop->foreground_input->shared : &dummy_obj.shm.input;
 
     set_clip_rectangle( desktop, NULL, SET_CURSOR_NOCLIP, 1 );
     desktop->foreground_input = input;
-    desktop->foreground_pid = process->id;
+    desktop->foreground_pid = is_desktop ? 0 : process->id;
 
     SHARED_WRITE_BEGIN( old_input_shm, input_shm_t )
     {
@@ -3813,7 +3829,8 @@
 
     reply->previous = desktop->foreground_input ? desktop->foreground_input->shared->active : 0;
     reply->send_msg_old = (reply->previous && desktop->foreground_input != queue->input);
-    set_foreground_input( desktop, thread->process, req->internal || !is_desktop ? thread->queue->input : NULL );
+    set_foreground_input( desktop, thread->process,
+                          req->internal || !is_desktop ? thread->queue->input : NULL, is_desktop );
     reply->send_msg_new = (desktop->foreground_input != queue->input);
 
 done:
